From c019bd99d0bf1ca0797e6bb21bb4b41aaed06ad0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: energie-teilen <noreply@energie-teilen.de>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:09:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(mako): market time base, market identifiers, EDIFACT
 syntax and MSCONS
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four layers that move quarter-hour values into the market, each with its
own correctness claim and each tested for it.

shared/market-time.ts — the German market runs on a LOCAL quarter-hour
grid, and that grid is not 96 intervals long. The last Sunday in March
has 92, the last Sunday in October has 100. The interval count is
derived from the elapsed time between two local midnights rather than
assumed, so nothing downstream can silently drop four intervals in
spring or double-count four in autumn. A full year still totals 35 040
because the two changes cancel, which is exactly why a year-level check
proves nothing; a test pins that trap. The repeated October hour is
distinguished for humans by an A/B label and on the wire by its UTC
offset. No timezone dependency: the IANA rules come from the platform
through Intl.

shared/market-ids.ts — MaLo, MeLo, BDEW code number and EIC. Format
rules need nobody's publication and are asserted plainly; check-digit
algorithms are somebody else's specification, so each is registered with
its own verified flag and a validator reports `algorithm_unverified`
rather than "valid" until that flag is set. Same gate the statutory
citations use.

shared/edifact.ts — ISO 9735 level A. Escaping is where naive
implementations break: an OBIS code contains the component separator, a
UTC offset contains the element separator, and a partner name can
contain either. Values never reach the wire unescaped, the parser
reverses it exactly, and the round trip is tested against payloads built
out of the reserved characters. UNT segment counts and the UNZ control
reference are computed from content, and verifyInterchange() re-checks
them by reading the produced message back — with tests that corrupt a
known-good message to establish the verifier would catch a broken
builder.

shared/mscons.ts — the metering-value message, assembled on the real
grid with identifiers validated before anything is sent. A 96-value
series for a clock-change day is refused with the reason named, rather
than padded or truncated. Substituted and estimated values carry their
own qualifier, because settlement treats them differently. The segment
profile is a published format description that changes on a release
cycle: it is registered unverified, the interchange is marked as a test
while it stays that way, and the result says which part is proven and
which is not.

Also: illustrativeDayProfile() takes the day's real interval count, and
the allocation module's resolution constants now come from the market
time base so there is one source of truth.

- GET  /api/v1/mako/grid
- POST /api/v1/mako/identifiers
- POST /api/v1/mako/mscons
- MarktkommunikationPanel: pick any of the three kinds of day, see the
  interval count, the labels around the transition, the generated
  message and its independent syntax check.

137 new tests; 485 total. Typecheck, tests and build clean.
